# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
# Node
node_modules/
dist/
# Env & secrets
# Matches at every level, so the per-service overrides 06-backend/.env and
# 07-frontend/.env are ignored too. Their committed companions —
# 06-backend/.env.example and 07-frontend/.env.example — are NOT ignored: they
# are the defaults docker-compose.yml loads, and they carry no real secret.
.env
.env.local
*.pem
# IDE / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
# Test artifacts
# `qa/e2e/…` is the PRE-RENAME path and has matched nothing since the numbered
# rename (the same stale-path family as ci-frontend.yml's `frontend` working
# directory). It mattered from 2026-08-11, when Playwright started actually
# RUNNING: every run writes 07-frontend/test-results/ and, on failure,
# 07-frontend/playwright-report/, and neither was ignored. The dead lines are
# kept for one commit's readability and should go with the next sweep.
qa/e2e/test-results/
qa/e2e/playwright-report/
07-frontend/test-results/
07-frontend/playwright-report/
# The DURABLE journey run receipt (DEF-113, 2026-08-15). It is deliberately NOT
# under test-results/: that directory is Playwright's `outputDir` and Playwright
# PURGES it at the start of every run, so with several journey lanes in one tree
# a genuine receipt vanished within nine seconds of the run that produced it and
# two wave-close runs reported "no run receipt" while a real one had existed.
# Still a run artifact, still never committed — one immutable shard per run under
# runs/, plus the merged view folded from them.
07-frontend/journey-evidence/
coverage/
htmlcov/

# Job artifacts (BE-07 export files; retention swept per BR-REP-018)
06-backend/var/

# Throwaway browser probes. The E2E lane writes these to poke a running app by
# hand — hardcoded executable paths, a fixture login, no Trace header, deleted
# the moment they have answered their question. They are working notes, not
# tests: a real journey lives in 07-frontend/e2e/ with its own header and is
# asserted against a FROZEN figure (PO ruling 2026-08-11, "prove against human
# clicking"). Ignored so a scratch file cannot drift into being treated as one.
07-frontend/probe*.mjs

# Agent worktrees — isolated CHECKOUTS of this repo, never source.
# Created when a lane needs its own copy because several lanes share one
# working tree (CLAUDE.md law 11: five commits crossed lanes on 2026-08-16,
# one of them deleting a shipped money fix). Committing a worktree would
# commit the repository inside itself.
.claude/worktrees/

# A lane's scratch while it drives a browser in the SHARED tree — server logs,
# a boot script, a throwaway probe. Appeared untracked on 2026-09-05 when the
# Work walkthrough booted a stack here rather than in a worktree. It is working
# residue, not source: committing it would put one lane's `backend.log` in every
# other lane's checkout, and the next run overwrites it anyway.
.claude/tmp/

# Build output from `pip install -e .` — generated, never source. It appeared
# untracked on 2026-08-16 when three lanes each built a py3.12 venv to run
# targeted backend tests. Committing it would put a generated file in the repo,
# which hard-won law 5 exists to prevent ("one generated file, committed once" —
# and this one should be committed ZERO times).
*.egg-info/

# The CR-028 lane's py3.12 test venv (the system python is 3.11, the backend
# requires >=3.12). Same law as *.egg-info above: a toolchain, never source.
.venv-ci/

# Vite's dep-optimizer cache. It appeared untracked on 2026-08-21 when a dev
# server was launched with its cwd in 06-backend during the browser walkthrough
# (DEF-528..533's harness) — same law-5 posture as *.egg-info: generated, never
# source, committed zero times.
.vite/

# Redis snapshot written wherever a redis-server is started from (a test-suite
# side effect, never repo material) — found untracked in 06-backend/ 2026-08-22.
dump.rdb

# Lane scratch, written INSIDE the shared checkout (found 2026-08-27 with 37
# entries: gate logs, a private GIT_INDEX_FILE per commit-tree run, a CSV
# working copy). None of it is repo material and all of it is one broad
# pathspec away from being committed by a lane that meant something else —
# which is CLAUDE.md law 11's mechanism with the safety off. The scratchpad the
# session is given lives outside the repo; this covers the one lanes keep
# creating in it anyway.
/scratchpad/

# ⚠ A FILENAME, NOT A PATTERN. `[&>.field]:sm:grid-rows-subgrid` is a Tailwind
# arbitrary-variant selector, and unquoted in a shell it is a GLOB — so a lane
# that pasted one into a command left bash's own error text redirected into a
# 35-byte file named after the fragment, in the repo root. Ignored rather than
# deleted, because deleting another lane's untracked file in a shared checkout
# is the thing DEF-934 was filed for. Quote the selector.
.field]:sm:grid-rows-subgrid

# ⚠ TWO MORE FILENAMES, NOT PATTERNS — same rule as the line above, same reason
# (DEF-934: deleting another lane's untracked file in a shared checkout is worse
# than leaving it). Both were sitting in the tree on 2026-08-28.
#
# `a.out` is 416 bytes of stray relocatable ELF, not even executable — a
# compiler invoked with no `-o` in the repo root.
a.out

# `test_RED_tmp.py` is the law-7 RED scaffold, and it is the one artefact of that
# law that MUST NOT be committed. Proving a guard red means running the real test
# against a pre-fix copy of the file it guards, so the scaffold is the permanent
# test with one line repointed at a copy under the session's own scratchpad — a
# path that exists on nobody else's machine. Committed, it is a test that fails
# for everyone but its author. The measured instance was byte-identical to
# `10-scripts/tests/test_def1182_reserved_blocks.py` apart from that single line.
10-scripts/tests/test_RED_tmp.py

# ⚠ THE BROWSER WALKTHROUGH'S OUTPUT, AND IT IS EVIDENCE RATHER THAN SOURCE.
# `10-scripts/cwp_portal_walkthrough.mjs` writes one full-page PNG per portal
# screen — ~800 KB a run, regenerated from scratch every time and meaningless
# once the build moves. The SCRIPT is committed and the pictures are not: what
# the next lane needs is the ability to take them again, against its own tree.
07-frontend/shots/

# ⚠ `SCRATCH/` — THE BILLING LANES' PROOF SHOTS, AND THE SAME CALL AS
# `07-frontend/shots/` TWENTY LINES UP. Four journey lanes wrote 33 full-page
# PNGs here on 2026-09-14 (4.1 MB) driving the bill run, the one-time bill, fix
# a bill and budget bills at 1440 and 1280. They are EVIDENCE rather than
# SOURCE: each is true of one build and meaningless once the screen moves, and
# nothing reads them back. The WALKTHROUGH SCRIPTS are committed —
# `10-scripts/cr101_fix_a_bill_walkthrough.mjs` and its siblings — so the next
# lane can take its own against its own tree, which is the thing that actually
# transfers. The conformance records cite these paths as where a shot WAS
# taken, not as a file the repo carries.
# ⚠ IT WAS ALREADY BEING TREATED THIS WAY AND NOBODY HAD SAID SO: zero files
# under `SCRATCH/` have ever been committed on this branch. Declaring it here
# turns a habit into a rule, and stops the working tree reading dirty for every
# lane that proves its work by looking at it.
SCRATCH/

# The move-out scenario suite's stderr log, written on EVERY run by
# `10-scripts/move_out_scenarios.sh:89` (`MOVE_OUT_STDERR`, defaulting to
# `06-backend/.move-out-scenarios-stderr.log`). It is 388 KB after one run of
# the 27 rows, and the suite is committed precisely so the next lane runs it
# against its own tree -- which means every lane that does produces this file
# and finds the working tree reading dirty. Same reasoning as `SCRATCH/`
# above: EVIDENCE of one run, true of one build, read back by nothing. The
# runner and the journey are the things that transfer.
.move-out-scenarios-stderr.log
# ...and its REPORT, written beside the log on every run by the same script.
# It was missed when the block above was written and the transfer block below,
# written later, ignores BOTH of its suite's files -- which is the asymmetry
# that left a lane's working tree reading dirty after an honest re-run.
.move-out-scenarios-report.txt

# The transfer scenario suite's two scratch files, written on EVERY run by
# `10-scripts/transfer_scenarios.sh`. Same reasoning as the move-out suite's
# stderr log above and `SCRATCH/` before it: the suite is committed precisely
# so the next lane runs it against its own tree, so every lane that does
# produces these and finds the working tree reading dirty. Evidence of one run,
# true of one build, read back by nothing -- the runner and the journey are
# what transfer.
.transfer-scenarios-report.txt
.transfer-scenarios-stderr.log
